;; Eight-pulse gong cycle with ugal melody, kempli and gong/kempur.
;; Each note is one kempli pulse. Pulse 9 is pulse 1 of the next cycle,
;; where the gong lands again; it is written out because the source
;; figure shows it. The ugal line continues ("etc.") beyond this.
;;
;; The source writes the melody in syllables (nang ning nong neng).
;; These are the selisir degrees, written here in short form by vowel:
;; nang = a, ning = i, nong = o, neng = e. Drawn lowest to highest, they
;; are four adjacent ugal keys: a1 i2 o2 e2. The figure does not give
;; the register; this places them in the middle of the ugal.

(piece "Eight-pulse gong cycle")

(use gong-kebyar)

;; pulse:      1  2  3  4  5  6  7  8  1
(part ugal    a1 o2 i2 e2 o2 a1 o2 i2 a1)
(part kempli  K  K  K  K  K  K  K  K  K)
(part gong    G  -  -  -  G  P  -  P  G)
